<!--
  Every page of this site a search engine should index, in the URL form that answers 200.

  Measured 2026-11-14 on the live site: the asset layer's default html_handling redirects
  /privacy.html → /privacy (307) and /project/cairn → /project/cairn/ (307). A sitemap that
  listed the redirecting form would hand every crawler a hop; these are the 200 forms. The
  store privacy address /privacy.html is unchanged and still resolves — it simply is not the
  URL search engines are pointed at.

  Left out on purpose: /turquoise/auth/, the landing page for the links in Turquoise's own
  e-mails, which carries <meta name="robots" content="noindex"> and is nobody's search result.
  /project/ is a 301 to /#work, so it is not a page any more.

  No <lastmod>, <changefreq> or <priority>. Google ignores the last two outright, and a lastmod
  written by hand goes stale the first time a page changes without this file being touched —
  a wrong date is read as a lie and the whole file is then distrusted. Eight pages are crawled
  in full anyway.

  test/sitemap.test.js derives this list from public/ and fails if a page is added, removed or
  renamed without this file following.
-->
